  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nFor the rest of today, a hot and muggy afternoon across the \nNorthland with temperatures warming well into the 80s and even \nlow 90s far inland. Closer to Lake Superior, brisk northeast \nwinds will keep temperatures cooler but will also drive high \nwaves and dangerous rip currents along the Twin Ports beaches - \na Beach Hazards Statement is in effect today. Areas of dense fog\nand will also linger into the afternoon, especially near the \nlake.\n\nHeading into tonight and Tuesday, high pressure begins to build\ninto our area, ushering in a drier and a slightly more \ncomfortable airmass. While daytime highs on Tuesday will remain \nunseasonably warm in the 80s to lower 90s, the oppressive \nhumidity will take a subtle break. Expect mostly sunny skies \nwith patchy smoke continuing to drift overhead. A stray \nafternoon shower or thunderstorm cannot be completely ruled out,\nbut the vast majority of the region will stay dry and pleasant.\nMarine fog may drift on and off shore but that will also help \nmoderate temperatures.\n\nThe quiet weather holds firm through Wednesday as the upper\nlevel ridge dominates the central United States. Highs will \nagain reach the mid 80s to lower 90s across northeast Minnesota \nand northwest Wisconsin. Overnight lows during this stretch will\nprovide some relief, dropping comfortably into the upper 50s \nand 60s.\n\nOur next significant weather maker arrives on Thursday and\nFriday as a shortwave trough and associated cold front \napproach from the west. This system will bring our next \nwidespread chance for showers and thunderstorms, and possibly \nsome severe threat too though shear looks a little weak at the \nmoment and could be the limiting factor. As southerly flow \nincreases ahead of the front, humidity levels will creep back \nup, maintaining high temperatures in the upper 80s despite the \nincreased cloud cover and precipitation chances.\n\nLooking ahead to the upcoming weekend, the Northland will remain \nin a somewhat stagnant weather pattern. Afternoon highs will \ngenerally stay in the 80s. With the main storm track positioned \nto our north, we can expect occasional, less organized waves of \nshowers and storms to dive across the area.\n\nLooking toward the first week of August, the Climate Prediction \nCenter indicates that the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes region \nwill likely experience above normal temperatures. Precipitation \nsignals are weaker, but slightly favor near to below normal \nprecipitation across the area as upper level ridging influences \nthe central CONUS.",
